s to be able to detect  quickly so the Advent of T2 results  early before culture has allowed us to  again intervene early with regard to  antibiotic support for what could be a  life-threatening infection if let go for  long enough to have the culture turn  positive over what often is 12 to 24  hours for that to occur  two out of ten had candida peroxillosis  one also had coincidentally with it  pseudomonas both patients were on  anaerobic coverage interestingly at the  time of this again to emphasize the  importance of anaerobes in terms of  trophic messages to the GI tract and the  immune system  and how important that is for the  control of infections in our population  one had enterococcus vcm one head staph  aureus and all of these positive culture  positive T2 results with negative  cultures happened relatively early in  the course of our patients we did trait  based on these T2 results would there  have been a difference in mortality or  the overall response for patients who  had been treated later based on cultural  results alone based on Modern therapy  now where we're de-emphasizing early  antibiotics it's hard to say but  certainly we're very happy to see this  early recognition and ability to treat  ahead of culture results  uh there were uh rarely a T2 negative  but culture positive result that  happened in our population we had one  patient with an E coli on culture that  didn't get found with the t2 bacterial  panel there was also strepmitus found in  the same patient which seemed to imply  that this was probably some sort of  bacterial translocation  this was a patient  um this was a patient who had a  complicated course with GI graft versus  host disease on and on multiple  antibiotics at the time that this study  was done which may have complicated the  interpretation  and finally as Dr Snyder had mentioned  during his presentation there are  certainly organisms that happen on the  t2 on on culture which are not currently  represented in the t2 panel which  include those listed here  um those some of which are important and  we'd like to see certainly included in  future versions of this there will  always be a relative balance of risk and  benefit for which of these organisms  plays a greater role in early morbidity  and mortality that need to be decided  quickly with regard to antibiotics  versus those that can comfortably be  found on culture and not make an overall  clinical difference for patients but  that will come from further  understanding and research I believe on  this system  so the benefits of the t2 system  we've certainly seen a rapid ability to  treatment focused organisms and prevent  broad spectrum sometimes more broader  Spectrum antibiotics which helps us to  preserve the relative uh health of the  microbiome which is again as I've  mentioned a critical issue in the age of  modern immunotherapy so it helps us to  prevent over treatment and it is a  relatively easy method of testing  notwithstanding as Dr Snyder has brought  up the need for peripheral culture and  the need for adequate blood supply for  the test  open questions with regards to the t2  system is how much we should trust  negative results in terms of making  decisions about earlier broad spectrum  antibiotics  polymicrobial interference with results  and it's very helpful for a more  targeted approach when positive early  and with that I will finish my section  of the presentation and ask for any  questions again I'd like to thank the  organizers and certainly our partner Dr  Snyder who helped to make this system  possible at our Institution  thank you so much Dr Snyder and Dr  Emmons for your informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you'd like to ask please do so now just  click on the ask a question box located  on the far left of your screen and we'll  answer as many of your questions as we  have time for  now let's get started  our first question  um does T